  "id": "sec-s10e01",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-s10e01.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "10.1",
  "title": "Episode 1: Solve Systems of Equations by Graphing",
  "body": " Episode 1: Solve Systems of Equations by Graphing   Episode 1 Learning Goals     Interpret a system of linear equations, and interpret a solution to a system    Solve a system of linear equations by graphing both equations and looking for the point of intersection       Episode 1 Key Terminology     System of equations    Solution to a system of equations       Episode 1 Key Symbols        A system of equations consists of two or more equations that need to be true simultaneously.    Equations can be collected into a system by enclosing them with a left curly bracket, for example: Remember that a single equation can be used to express a desired result or a known fact. So a system of equations can be used to represent multiple desired results, or multiple known facts.   A rectangle has an unknown length L and a width W. You want to make a rectangle with a perimeter of 20 inches and an area of 25 square inches. Express this situation using a system of equations.  Solution: First, you want the perimeter to be 20 inches. Using the perimeter formula for a rectangle, this becomes the equation: Second, you want the area to be 25 square inches. Using the area formula for a rectangle, this becomes the equation: Because I want both of these things to be true at the same time, I write both equations in a system: A solution to a single equation is something that makes the equation true. It tells you how to achieve the result that the equation represents.  A solution to a system of equations is something that makes ALL the equations true at the same time. It tells you how to simultaneously achieve all the results that the equations represent.    Verify that represents a solution to the system   I need to be able to plug and into BOTH equations and make BOTH equations true. Plugging the values into the first equation: This is true, so and is a solution to the first equation.  Let’s plug the values into the equation: This is true, so and is also a solution to the second equation.  Because , was a solution to both equations, it is a solution to the system of equations.  In other words, to make a rectangle that has a perimeter of 20 inches and an area of 25 inches, you should make a rectangle that has a length of 5 inches and a width of 5 inches.   Remember that the points on the graph of an equation correspond to the solutions to that equation. So, if a point is on the graph of two equations, it must be a solution to both equations.   How to Solve a System of Equations by Graphing:    Graph the equations on the system. Make sure to draw both graphs on the same xy-plane.    Look for points where the graphs intersect (points on the graph of both equations).    The point of intersection is the solution to the system.      Solve the system of equations:   Solution: We start by graphing both equations.  The first equation, , has a slope of and a y-intercept of . We use the techniques of Season 09 to quickly graph this equation.   a graph of a y = -2x + 1    f(x) = -2*x+1         The second equation, , has a slope of and a y-intercept of . We use the techniques of Season 09 to quickly graph this equation too.   a graph of a y = -2x + 1 and y = x-2    f(x) = -2*x+1  g(x) = x-2        (1, -1)       Notice that the point lies on both lines; it is the point where the two lines intersect. So, is the solution to the system.    Solve the system of equations:   Solution: We start by graphing both equations.  The first equation is not currently written in slope-intercept form, so we start by solving for . Now we can see the first equation has a slope of and a y-intercept of , which we can use to graph the equation quickly.   a graph of a y = -x+2    f(x) = -x+2         The second equation is also not written in slope-intercept form, so we start by solving for : Now we can see that the second equation has a slope of and a y-intercept of , which we can use to graph the equation quickly:   a graph of a y = -x+2 and y = x-2    f(x) = -x+2  g(x) = x-2        (2, 0)       Notice that the point lies on both lines; it is the point where the two lines intersect. So, is the solution to the system.    Season 10, Episode 1 Practice Problems    Check to see if the given point is a solution to the following systems of equations                                  Solve the following systems by graphing.         blank xy-plane                  blank xy-plane                  blank xy-plane                  blank xy-plane                  blank xy-plane              "
